// New articles, 15 November 2026 (part 9): controversial questions in society and science. Current facts checked 15 November 2026.
ADDART({id:"climatedenial",t:"Climate Change Denial",c:"Science",i:"🌡️",w:"Climate change denial",r:["climchange","misinformation","conspiracy","scimeth","propaganda"],
p:[["Rejecting the science","Climate change denial is the rejection or downplaying of the scientific evidence on global warming."],["Manufactured doubt","Some fossil fuel companies funded campaigns to cast doubt on climate science."],["Overwhelming consensus","Over 97 percent of climate scientists agree that humans are warming the planet."]],
l:"Climate change denial is the rejection, dismissal or unjustified doubt of the scientific consensus that the Earth is warming and that human activities, mainly burning fossil fuels, are the main cause. It ranges from denying that warming is happening at all, to accepting warming but denying humans cause it, to accepting both but claiming the effects will be harmless or that action is pointless. Studies of scientific papers have found that more than 97 percent, and in later studies over 99 percent, of climate research agrees on human-caused warming. Historians and journalists have documented how some fossil fuel companies, whose own scientists studied climate change from the 1970s, funded groups that publicly questioned the science, using strategies similar to those once used by the tobacco industry. Denial has slowed action on climate change in some countries. Today, outright denial is less common than arguments about how quickly and at what cost to cut emissions, which are legitimate subjects of debate.",
s:[["What the science says","Scientists have understood since the 19th century that carbon dioxide traps heat. Measurements show that carbon dioxide levels have risen by about 50 percent since before the Industrial Revolution and that global temperatures have risen by about 1.3 °C. 2024 was the first calendar year more than 1.5 °C warmer than pre-industrial times."],["Manufacturing doubt","Internal documents published in 2015 showed that Exxon scientists had studied and accurately predicted warming in the late 1970s and 1980s. Industry groups such as the Global Climate Coalition (1989–2002) lobbied against limits on emissions. The historians Naomi Oreskes and Erik Conway described these tactics in their 2010 book Merchants of Doubt."],["Common claims","Common denial arguments include that the climate has always changed, that warming has stopped, that the Sun is responsible, or that scientists are faking data for funding. Scientists have answered each: past changes had natural causes, warming continues, solar activity has been flat or declining, and independent datasets from many countries agree."],["Politics and public opinion","In some countries, especially the United States, views on climate change divide sharply along political lines. In 2009, hacked emails from a British climate research unit were used to accuse scientists of fraud in what became known as ‘Climategate’; eight investigations found no fraud. The United States left the Paris Agreement in 2020 and again in January 2026."]],
k:[["Scientific consensus","Over 97% of climate papers; later studies over 99%"],["Warming so far","About 1.3 °C since pre-industrial times"],["First year above 1.5 °C","2024"],["Key book","Merchants of Doubt (2010)"],["‘Climategate’","2009; investigations found no fraud"]],
d:[["1896","Svante Arrhenius calculates the warming effect of CO₂"],["1977–82","Exxon scientists study climate change"],["1988","IPCC created"],["1989","Global Climate Coalition formed"],["2009","‘Climategate’ email controversy"],["2015","Paris Agreement; Exxon documents published"],["2024","First year above 1.5 °C"]],
s2:[["Denial, skepticism and debate","Scientists distinguish healthy skepticism, which questions claims and tests them with evidence, from denial, which rejects evidence that contradicts a fixed belief. Debates about the best policies, such as carbon taxes, nuclear power or adaptation, involve values and economics and are part of normal democratic argument."],["Talking about climate","Research suggests that people respond better to messages from trusted members of their own communities and to practical solutions than to fear alone. Showing that most scientists agree, sometimes called the ‘consensus gap’, can shift opinions."]],
sp:["The Keeling Curve","Since 1958, scientists at Mauna Loa Observatory in Hawaii have measured carbon dioxide in the air continuously. The resulting graph, called the Keeling Curve after Charles David Keeling, shows a steady rise with a yearly zigzag as plants in the Northern Hemisphere grow and decay."],
g:[["Consensus","General agreement among experts"],["Fossil fuels","Coal, oil and gas, formed from ancient life"],["Skepticism","Questioning claims and asking for evidence"],["Emissions","Gases released into the air"]],
f:["Carbon dioxide levels have risen about 50 percent since pre-industrial times.","Exxon’s own scientists predicted global warming in the late 1970s.","2024 was the first full year more than 1.5 °C above pre-industrial temperatures."],
fun:"Eunice Foote, an American scientist, showed in 1856 that carbon dioxide traps heat from the Sun, years before the better-known experiments of John Tyndall."});

ADDART({id:"cannabis",t:"Cannabis Legalization",c:"Society",i:"🌿",w:"Legality of cannabis",r:["drugtrade","law","mental","tobacco","medicinalplants"],
p:[["A changing law","Cannabis, or marijuana, is illegal in most countries but legal in a growing number."],["Medical and recreational","Some places allow it only as medicine, others for adult recreational use."],["Health debate","Supporters and opponents disagree about risks, crime and public health."]],
l:"Cannabis, also called marijuana, is a plant whose dried flowers and leaves contain THC, a chemical that alters mood and perception, and CBD, which does not cause a ‘high’. It is the most widely used illegal drug in the world. International treaties since the 1960s have required countries to ban it except for medical and scientific uses, and many countries still impose harsh penalties, including the death penalty for trafficking in some. Since the 2010s, a growing number of places have legalized adult recreational use, including Uruguay in 2013, Canada in 2018 and Germany in 2024, as well as about half of US states, while many more allow medical cannabis. In April 2026, the US government moved state-licensed medical cannabis to a less strict legal category. Supporters argue that legalization reduces arrests, weakens criminal gangs and raises taxes; opponents warn of addiction, mental health risks, especially for young people, and impaired driving. Research on the effects of legalization is ongoing.",
s:[["History","Cannabis has been used for fiber (hemp), medicine and rituals for thousands of years in Asia and elsewhere. Many countries banned it in the early 20th century; the UN Single Convention of 1961 placed it among the most tightly controlled drugs. In 2020 the UN removed cannabis from the category of drugs considered especially dangerous with little medical value."],["Legalization","Uruguay became the first country to fully legalize recreational cannabis in 2013, followed by Canada nationwide in 2018. Malta legalized home growing in 2021, and Germany allowed adults to possess and grow limited amounts from April 2024. Thailand decriminalized cannabis in 2022 but restricted it to medical use again in 2025 after a boom in cannabis shops."],["Health effects","Medical cannabis and cannabis-based medicines can help with some conditions, such as certain forms of epilepsy, nausea from chemotherapy and chronic pain. Risks include dependence, impaired memory and coordination, and, for heavy users who start young, a higher risk of psychosis. Modern products often contain much more THC than in the past."],["The debate","Supporters say prohibition has caused mass arrests, especially of minorities, and handed profits to criminals. Opponents fear that legalization makes cannabis seem harmless, increases use among teenagers and creates a powerful new industry. Early studies of places that legalized show mixed results on youth use and road safety."]],
k:[["Main active chemical","THC (tetrahydrocannabinol)"],["First country to legalize","Uruguay, 2013"],["Canada legalized","October 2018"],["Germany","April 2024"],["US federal change","Medical cannabis moved to Schedule III, April 2026"]],
d:[["1961","UN Single Convention controls cannabis"],["1996","California legalizes medical cannabis"],["2013","Uruguay legalizes cannabis"],["2018","Canada legalizes cannabis nationwide"],["2020","UN reclassifies cannabis"],["2024","Germany partially legalizes cannabis"],["2026","US reschedules state-licensed medical cannabis"]],
s2:[["Hemp","Hemp is a variety of cannabis with very little THC. Its strong fibers have been used for rope, sails, paper and clothing for thousands of years, and it is grown legally in many countries for fiber, seeds and oil."],["Young people","Health experts agree that cannabis use is riskier for teenagers because the brain continues developing until the mid-20s. Most places that have legalized cannabis set a minimum age of 18 to 21."]],
sp:["Amsterdam’s coffee shops","Since 1976, the Netherlands has tolerated the sale of small amounts of cannabis in licensed ‘coffee shops’, even though it remained technically illegal. The policy aimed to separate cannabis users from markets for harder drugs."],
g:[["THC","The chemical in cannabis that causes a ‘high’"],["CBD","Cannabidiol, a non-intoxicating chemical in cannabis"],["Legalize","To make legal"],["Decriminalize","To stop treating something as a crime, while not fully legalizing it"]],
f:["Uruguay was the first country to legalize recreational cannabis.","Hemp has been grown for fiber for thousands of years.","Modern cannabis is often much stronger than in the past."],
fun:"In 2018 Canada became the first G7 country, and the second country in the world, to legalize recreational cannabis nationwide."});

ADDART({id:"childlabor",t:"Child Labor",c:"Society",i:"🧒",w:"Child labor",r:["hrights","childsoldiers","trafficking","fastfashion","education"],
p:[["Children at work","Child labor is work that harms children or keeps them from school."],["138 million children","About one in twelve children worldwide were in child labor in 2024."],["Laws and progress","Child labor has fallen sharply but has not been eliminated."]],
l:"Child labor is work done by children that is harmful to their health, safety or development or that interferes with their schooling. It does not include light chores or age-appropriate work that does not harm children. According to the International Labour Organization and UNICEF, about 138 million children aged 5 to 17 were in child labor in 2024, and 54 million of them did hazardous work. Most child laborers work in agriculture, often on family farms, but others work in mines, factories, domestic service, street trading and fishing. Poverty, lack of schools, debt and conflict are the main causes. Child labor was common in Europe and North America during the Industrial Revolution until reforms limited it. The worst forms, such as slavery, trafficking and use in armed conflict, are banned by an international convention ratified by every UN member. The number of child laborers has fallen by more than 100 million since 2000.",
s:[["The Industrial Revolution","In 19th-century Britain, children as young as five worked long hours in textile mills, coal mines and as chimney sweeps. Reformers such as Lord Shaftesbury campaigned for change, and Factory Acts from 1833 limited children’s working hours. In the United States, photographer Lewis Hine documented child workers in the early 1900s, helping to win new laws."],["Child labor today","Most child laborers are in sub-Saharan Africa and Asia. Children harvest cocoa in West Africa, mine cobalt and mica, weave carpets, work in brick kilns and serve as domestic workers. Many work alongside their families out of necessity, and girls often carry heavy household burdens."],["Laws","The ILO’s Minimum Age Convention of 1973 sets basic minimum ages for work, and its Worst Forms of Child Labour Convention of 1999 became, in 2020, the first ILO convention ratified by every member country. The UN Sustainable Development Goals aimed to end child labor by 2025, a target that was missed."],["Solutions","Free and compulsory schooling, family income support, school meals, birth registration and enforcement of labor laws all reduce child labor. Companies are increasingly expected to check their supply chains. Some experts warn that sudden bans without support can push children into worse, hidden work."]],
k:[["Children in child labor (2024)","About 138 million"],["In hazardous work","About 54 million"],["Main sector","Agriculture"],["Universal convention","ILO Convention 182 (1999)"],["World Day Against Child Labour","12 June"]],
d:[["1833","British Factory Act limits child labor"],["1908","Lewis Hine begins photographing child workers"],["1938","US Fair Labor Standards Act"],["1973","ILO Minimum Age Convention"],["1995","Activist Iqbal Masih killed in Pakistan"],["1999","Convention on the worst forms of child labor"],["2020","Convention 182 ratified by all ILO members"]],
s2:[["Iqbal Masih","Iqbal Masih was sold into bonded labor at a carpet factory in Pakistan at age four. After escaping at about ten, he campaigned internationally against child slavery, receiving a human rights award in 1994. He was shot dead in 1995 at about age 12, and schools have been named after him around the world."],["Cocoa and chocolate","Much of the world’s cocoa comes from small farms in Côte d’Ivoire and Ghana, where studies have found over a million children working in cocoa farming, many in hazardous tasks. Chocolate companies have pledged to end child labor in their supply chains but have repeatedly missed their own deadlines."]],
sp:["Lewis Hine’s photographs","Lewis Hine, a teacher turned photographer, sometimes disguised himself as a fire inspector or Bible salesman to get into factories. His thousands of photos of children in mills, mines and fields shocked the American public."],
g:[["Child labor","Work that harms children or stops them going to school"],["Hazardous work","Work that endangers health or safety"],["Bonded labor","Work to pay off a debt, often never-ending"],["Supply chain","The steps in making and delivering a product"]],
f:["About 138 million children were in child labor in 2024.","Most child laborers work in agriculture.","Child labor has fallen by more than 100 million since 2000."],
fun:"Kailash Satyarthi, an Indian activist who has freed tens of thousands of children from forced labor, shared the 2014 Nobel Peace Prize with Malala Yousafzai."});

ADDART({id:"whaling",t:"Whaling",c:"Nature",i:"🐋",w:"Whaling",r:["whale","conservation","fish","pacific","japancountry"],
p:[["Hunting whales","Whaling is the hunting of whales for meat, oil and other products."],["Near extinction","Industrial whaling drove many whale species close to extinction."],["A global ban","Commercial whaling was banned in 1986, but Japan, Norway and Iceland still hunt."]],
l:"Whaling is the hunting of whales, mainly for their meat, blubber and oil. People have hunted whales for thousands of years, but industrial whaling in the 19th and 20th centuries, using steamships, explosive harpoons and factory ships, killed millions of whales and drove species such as the blue whale and the humpback close to extinction. In 1946 whaling nations formed the International Whaling Commission (IWC), which in 1982 voted for a moratorium on commercial whaling that took effect in 1986. Many whale populations have since partly recovered. Japan continued whaling under a ‘scientific research’ exemption until the International Court of Justice ruled against its Antarctic program in 2014; it left the IWC in 2019 and resumed commercial whaling in its own waters. Norway and Iceland also hunt whales commercially. Indigenous communities in places such as Alaska, Greenland and Chukotka are allowed limited subsistence whaling. Supporters cite tradition and sustainable use; opponents argue whaling is cruel and unnecessary.",
s:[["History","Basque whalers hunted in the Atlantic by the 11th century. In the 18th and 19th centuries, whale oil lit lamps and greased machines, and whalebone stiffened corsets; New England whaling inspired Herman Melville’s novel Moby-Dick (1851). The invention of the explosive harpoon gun in the 1860s let whalers catch faster species such as blue and fin whales."],["Industrial whaling","In the 20th century, factory ships processed whales at sea, and the Antarctic became the main hunting ground. About 2.9 million large whales were killed in the 20th century, and some populations fell by over 90 percent."],["The moratorium","Environmental campaigns from the 1970s, such as ‘Save the Whales’, built support for a ban. The IWC moratorium took effect in 1986. Norway objected and resumed commercial hunting in 1993; Iceland resumed in 2006. Japan’s scientific program was criticized as commercial whaling in disguise."],["Whaling today","After leaving the IWC, Japan resumed commercial whaling in its own waters on 1 July 2019 and in 2024 added fin whales to its catch; its 2026 quota was 412 whales. Norway raised its quota for 2026, and Iceland resumed fin whaling in June 2026 after a pause. Demand for whale meat is small and falling in most countries."]],
k:[["International body","International Whaling Commission (1946)"],["Moratorium on commercial whaling","Since 1986"],["Countries hunting commercially","Japan, Norway, Iceland"],["ICJ ruling against Japan","31 March 2014"],["Japan left IWC","2019"]],
d:[["1851","Moby-Dick published"],["1860s","Explosive harpoon gun invented"],["1946","International Whaling Commission founded"],["1986","Moratorium on commercial whaling takes effect"],["2014","ICJ rules against Japan’s Antarctic whaling"],["2019","Japan resumes commercial whaling"],["2026","Iceland resumes fin whaling"]],
s2:[["Whale watching","Whale watching has become a major industry, attracting millions of tourists a year in places such as Iceland, Canada, Australia and the Dominican Republic. Many coastal communities now earn more from watching whales than they ever did from hunting them."],["Recovery","Since the moratorium, humpback whales in many regions have recovered strongly, and the western South Atlantic humpback population is close to its pre-whaling size. Blue whales remain endangered, and whales still face threats from ship strikes, fishing nets, noise and climate change."]],
sp:["Save the Whales","The ‘Save the Whales’ campaign of the 1970s was one of the first global environmental movements. Activists in small boats placed themselves between harpoons and whales, and recordings of humpback whale songs, released as an album in 1970, helped people see whales as intelligent, singing animals."],
g:[["Moratorium","A temporary ban"],["Blubber","The thick layer of fat under a whale’s skin"],["Harpoon","A spear-like weapon used to hunt whales"],["Subsistence","Hunting or farming to feed one’s own community"]],
f:["About 2.9 million large whales were killed in the 20th century.","Commercial whaling has been banned since 1986.","Japan, Norway and Iceland still hunt whales commercially."],
fun:"The 1970 album Songs of the Humpback Whale sold over 100,000 copies and helped launch the ‘Save the Whales’ movement."});

ADDART({id:"trophyhunting",t:"Trophy Hunting",c:"Nature",i:"🦁",w:"Trophy hunting",r:["conservation","lion","elephant","whaling","zoos"],
p:[["Hunting for trophies","Trophy hunting is hunting wild animals and keeping parts of them as trophies."],["Conservation or cruelty?","Supporters say fees fund conservation; opponents call it cruel and harmful."],["Cecil the lion","The killing of a famous lion in 2015 caused worldwide outrage."]],
l:"Trophy hunting is the hunting of wild animals for sport, in which the hunter keeps parts of the animal, such as the head, horns, tusks or skin, as a trophy. Wealthy hunters, often from North America and Europe, pay large fees to hunt species such as lions, elephants, leopards, rhinos and wild sheep in countries including South Africa, Namibia, Zimbabwe, Tanzania and Pakistan. Supporters argue that well-regulated trophy hunting brings money to remote areas, pays for wildlife protection and gives local communities a reason to keep wild animals rather than convert land to farms. Opponents argue that killing animals for pleasure is unethical, that hunting targets the strongest animals, that money often fails to reach communities and that corruption undermines regulation. The 2015 killing of Cecil, a well-known lion in Zimbabwe, by an American dentist sparked global outrage and led some airlines and countries to ban trophy imports. Scientists remain divided about its overall effect on conservation.",
s:[["History","Big-game hunting became popular among European colonial officials and wealthy travelers in Africa and India in the 19th and early 20th centuries, contributing to declines in some species. Modern trophy hunting is regulated by permits, quotas and the international CITES treaty, which controls trade in parts of endangered animals."],["The conservation argument","In Namibia, community conservancies earn income from both tourism and trophy hunting, and populations of some species have grown. In Pakistan, fees for hunting a small number of markhor, a wild goat, funded local protection, and the species’ conservation status improved. Supporters say banning hunting could remove funding for land that would otherwise be lost."],["The case against","Critics argue that few hunting fees reach local people, that quotas are sometimes set too high, and that photographic tourism can earn more without killing animals. Removing large males can disrupt animal groups; for lions, killing a pride male can lead to the deaths of cubs. Many people oppose trophy hunting on moral grounds."],["Bans and debates","Kenya banned sport hunting in 1977. Botswana banned trophy hunting in 2014 but lifted the ban in 2019, saying elephant numbers were causing conflict with farmers. Some Western countries have debated or introduced bans on importing trophies, while African governments have argued that such decisions should be made by the countries where the animals live."]],
k:[["Common target species","Lions, elephants, leopards, rhinos, wild sheep"],["Key treaty","CITES (1973)"],["Cecil the lion killed","July 2015, Zimbabwe"],["Kenya’s ban","1977"],["Botswana","Ban 2014–2019"]],
d:[["1973","CITES treaty signed"],["1977","Kenya bans sport hunting"],["1998","Namibia creates its first community conservancies"],["2014","Botswana bans trophy hunting"],["2015","Cecil the lion killed"],["2019","Botswana lifts its ban"]],
s2:[["Canned hunting","In ‘canned hunting’, animals, often lions bred in captivity, are shot in fenced areas where they cannot escape. It is widely condemned, including by many hunters. South Africa announced plans in 2021 to end captive lion breeding."],["Human–wildlife conflict","In many rural areas, elephants destroy crops and lions kill livestock, and people sometimes die. Conservationists say any policy, whether hunting or tourism, must benefit the people who live alongside dangerous animals."]],
sp:["Cecil","Cecil was a black-maned lion in Zimbabwe’s Hwange National Park, wearing a GPS collar for a university study. In July 2015 he was lured out of the park and killed by an American hunter with a bow. The story went viral, and more than 40 airlines banned trophy shipments."],
g:[["Trophy","A part of an animal kept to remember a hunt"],["Quota","A limit on how many animals may be hunted"],["CITES","The treaty controlling trade in endangered species"],["Conservancy","An area managed by local people to protect wildlife"]],
f:["Kenya has banned sport hunting since 1977.","Cecil the lion was wearing a research collar when he was killed.","Botswana is home to the world’s largest elephant population."],
fun:"After Cecil’s death, donations to the Oxford University team that had been studying him passed one million dollars within weeks."});

ADDART({id:"taxhavens",t:"Tax Havens",c:"Society",i:"🏝️",w:"Tax haven",r:["taxes","billionaires","organizedcrime","journalism","inequality"],
p:[["Hiding money offshore","Tax havens are places with very low taxes and strict secrecy."],["Leaks exposed them","The Panama Papers and other leaks revealed how the rich hide wealth."],["A global minimum tax","Many countries agreed in 2021 to a minimum 15 percent tax on large companies."]],
l:"A tax haven is a country or territory that offers very low or zero taxes and often financial secrecy to foreign individuals and companies. Well-known examples include the Cayman Islands, the British Virgin Islands, Bermuda, Luxembourg, Switzerland, Ireland and Singapore, though definitions are disputed. People and companies use tax havens legally to reduce taxes, but they can also be used for tax evasion, money laundering, hiding stolen money and avoiding sanctions. Economists estimate that trillions of dollars of private wealth are held offshore, and that multinational companies shift hundreds of billions of dollars of profits to low-tax places each year, reducing tax revenue for other countries, including developing ones. Leaks such as the Panama Papers in 2016 and the Pandora Papers in 2021 exposed the offshore dealings of politicians, celebrities and criminals. In response, countries began automatically sharing bank information, and in 2021 more than 130 countries agreed to a global minimum corporate tax of 15 percent.",
s:[["How tax havens work","Tax havens offer low tax rates, secret bank accounts and easy creation of ‘shell companies’ that exist only on paper. A company might register its valuable patents in a low-tax country and charge its branches elsewhere for using them, shifting profits. Wealthy individuals may hold assets through trusts and companies that hide who really owns them."],["The leaks","The Panama Papers, 11.5 million documents leaked from the law firm Mossack Fonseca and published on 3 April 2016 by the International Consortium of Investigative Journalists, revealed offshore companies linked to world leaders; Iceland’s prime minister resigned within days. The Paradise Papers (2017) and Pandora Papers (2021) followed."],["Reforms","Since 2017, more than 100 countries have automatically exchanged information on foreigners’ bank accounts, weakening traditional banking secrecy, including in Switzerland. The OECD’s global minimum tax took effect in many countries from 2024, though in 2025 the G7 agreed to exempt US-based companies from parts of it."],["Legal or wrong?","Tax avoidance using legal methods is different from tax evasion, which is illegal. Defenders say low-tax countries have the right to set their own rates and that companies have a duty to their shareholders. Critics argue that profit-shifting is unfair to ordinary taxpayers and smaller businesses and deprives public services of funding."]],
k:[["Examples","Cayman Islands, British Virgin Islands, Bermuda, Luxembourg"],["Panama Papers","3 April 2016, 11.5 million documents"],["Pandora Papers","October 2021"],["Global minimum tax","15% (agreed 2021)"],["Journalists’ network","International Consortium of Investigative Journalists"]],
d:[["1934","Swiss banking secrecy law"],["2014","LuxLeaks reveal company tax deals in Luxembourg"],["2016","Panama Papers published"],["2017","Automatic bank information exchange begins"],["2021","Pandora Papers; global minimum tax agreed"],["2024","Minimum tax takes effect in many countries"]],
s2:[["Who loses?","Studies by the IMF and academic economists suggest that developing countries lose a larger share of their tax revenue to profit-shifting than rich countries. This money could pay for schools, hospitals and roads."],["Tax havens and crime","Shell companies in secretive places have been used to hide money from corruption, drug trafficking and fraud. Many countries now require public or official registers of who really owns companies, known as beneficial ownership registers."]],
sp:["The Panama Papers investigation","More than 370 journalists from about 80 countries worked in secret for a year on the Panama Papers, using special software to search the 2.6 terabytes of data. It was one of the largest collaborations in journalism history and won a Pulitzer Prize."],
g:[["Tax haven","A place with very low taxes and financial secrecy"],["Shell company","A company with no real business, often used to hold money"],["Tax avoidance","Legally reducing taxes"],["Tax evasion","Illegally not paying taxes"]],
f:["The Panama Papers contained 11.5 million documents.","Iceland’s prime minister resigned after the Panama Papers.","More than 130 countries agreed to a 15 percent minimum tax on big companies."],
fun:"The British Virgin Islands, with about 40,000 residents, has been home to hundreds of thousands of registered companies, many times more companies than people."});

ADDART({id:"prisons",t:"Prisons and Mass Incarceration",c:"Society",i:"🔒",w:"Incarceration",r:["law","police","deathpenalty","torture","racism"],
p:[["Locking people up","Prisons hold people convicted of crimes or awaiting trial."],["Over 11 million prisoners","More than 11 million people are imprisoned worldwide."],["Punishment or rehabilitation?","Countries differ in whether prisons mainly punish or help people change."]],
l:"A prison is a place where people are held as punishment for crimes or while awaiting trial. More than 11.5 million people were imprisoned worldwide in the mid-2020s. Prisons aim to punish, protect the public, deter crime and rehabilitate offenders, and countries balance these aims very differently. The United States has the largest prison population, about 1.8 million people, and one of the highest imprisonment rates, a situation critics call mass incarceration, while El Salvador’s rate became the world’s highest after a crackdown on gangs from 2022. Debates focus on whether long sentences reduce crime, racial and economic inequalities in who goes to prison, overcrowding, prison conditions, private prisons and the high rates at which former prisoners reoffend. Some countries, such as Norway, emphasize rehabilitation, with prisons designed to resemble normal life, and have some of the lowest reoffending rates. Others stress strict punishment, arguing it deters crime and reflects justice for victims.",
s:[["History","For most of history, prisons mainly held people awaiting trial or punishment, which was often physical. In the late 18th century, reformers such as John Howard in England argued for humane prisons, and imprisonment itself became the main punishment. Jeremy Bentham designed the Panopticon to allow constant surveillance of prisoners."],["Mass incarceration in the United States","The US prison population grew about fivefold from the 1970s to 2008, driven by the ‘war on drugs’, mandatory minimum sentences and ‘three strikes’ laws. Black Americans are imprisoned at much higher rates than white Americans. Since 2010 the numbers have fallen somewhat as states reformed sentencing."],["El Salvador","In 2022, El Salvador declared a state of emergency against gangs and arrested more than 80,000 people. Murders fell sharply and the policy was popular, but human rights groups reported arbitrary arrests, deaths in custody and lack of fair trials. Its Terrorism Confinement Center, opened in 2023, can hold 40,000 people."],["The Nordic model","Norway’s Halden prison, opened in 2010, has private rooms, kitchens and job training, and guards work closely with prisoners. Norway’s two-year reoffending rate is about 20 percent, far lower than in many countries. Critics argue such systems are expensive and may seem too lenient to victims."]],
k:[["Prisoners worldwide","Over 11.5 million"],["Largest prison population","United States, about 1.8 million"],["Highest imprisonment rate","El Salvador (since 2023)"],["Norway reoffending rate","About 20% within two years"],["UN rules for prisoners","Nelson Mandela Rules (2015)"]],
d:[["1777","John Howard publishes The State of the Prisons"],["1791","Bentham designs the Panopticon"],["1971","Stanford prison experiment"],["2008","US prison population peaks"],["2010","Norway’s Halden prison opens"],["2015","UN adopts the Nelson Mandela Rules"],["2022","El Salvador’s state of emergency begins"]],
s2:[["Alternatives to prison","Alternatives include probation, community service, electronic monitoring, drug courts and restorative justice, in which offenders meet victims and make amends. Research suggests these can reduce reoffending for many non-violent offenders and cost much less than prison."],["Prison conditions","Overcrowding is common worldwide, with many prisons holding far more people than they were built for. The UN’s Nelson Mandela Rules, named after the South African leader who spent 27 years in prison, set minimum standards for the treatment of prisoners."]],
sp:["Robben Island","Nelson Mandela spent 18 of his 27 years in prison on Robben Island, off Cape Town, breaking rocks in a limestone quarry. The former prison is now a museum and UNESCO World Heritage Site, where former political prisoners guide visitors."],
g:[["Incarceration","Imprisonment"],["Rehabilitation","Helping someone change and return to society"],["Recidivism","Reoffending after release"],["Mandatory minimum","A minimum sentence set by law"]],
f:["More than 11.5 million people are imprisoned worldwide.","The United States has about one in six of the world’s prisoners.","Norway’s prisons are known for low reoffending rates."],
fun:"Alcatraz, the famous former island prison in San Francisco Bay, operated for only 29 years, from 1934 to 1963, but became one of the best-known prisons in the world."});

ADDART({id:"torture",t:"Torture",c:"Society",i:"⛓️",w:"Torture",r:["hrights","inquisition","prisons","sept11","law"],
p:[["Deliberate cruelty","Torture is deliberately inflicting severe pain to punish, intimidate or extract information."],["Banned everywhere","International law bans torture in all circumstances."],["Still used","Human rights groups report torture in many countries today."]],
l:"Torture is the deliberate infliction of severe physical or mental pain or suffering, usually by officials, to obtain information or confessions, to punish, or to intimidate. It was a legal part of criminal procedure in many societies for centuries, including in medieval and early modern Europe, until reformers argued it was cruel and produced false confessions. The UN Convention against Torture, adopted in 1984, bans torture absolutely, even in wartime or emergencies, and has been ratified by most countries. Despite this, human rights groups report torture in many places. After the September 11 attacks, the United States used ‘enhanced interrogation techniques’, including waterboarding, on terrorism suspects held in secret CIA prisons and at Guantanamo Bay; a 2014 Senate report concluded they were brutal and ineffective. Debates continue over hypothetical ‘ticking bomb’ scenarios, but research and experience show that torture often produces unreliable information and causes lasting harm to victims and societies.",
s:[["History","Ancient Greek and Roman law allowed torture of slaves as witnesses. Medieval European courts and the Inquisition used it to obtain confessions. In the 18th century, writers such as Cesare Beccaria and Voltaire attacked it, and Prussia abolished judicial torture in 1754, followed by most of Europe."],["International law","The Universal Declaration of Human Rights (1948) states that no one shall be subjected to torture. The Geneva Conventions forbid torturing prisoners of war, and the Convention against Torture came into force in 1987. Evidence obtained by torture is generally inadmissible in court. 26 June is the International Day in Support of Victims of Torture."],["The ‘war on terror’","The Guantanamo Bay detention camp opened in January 2002, eventually holding about 780 men, most never charged. In 2004, photographs of abuse at Abu Ghraib prison in Iraq caused outrage. The US Senate Intelligence Committee’s 2014 report described CIA interrogations in detail. As of 2026, 15 detainees remained at Guantanamo."],["Why it fails","Interrogation experts say people in extreme pain will say anything to make it stop, including false information. Rapport-based interviewing is considered more effective. Torture also harms those who carry it out, damages trust in institutions and is used by opponents as propaganda."]],
k:[["UN Convention against Torture","Adopted 1984, in force 1987"],["Day for victims of torture","26 June"],["Guantanamo opened","11 January 2002"],["Guantanamo detainees (2026)","15"],["US Senate torture report","December 2014"]],
d:[["1754","Prussia abolishes judicial torture"],["1948","Universal Declaration of Human Rights bans torture"],["1984","UN Convention against Torture adopted"],["2002","Guantanamo detention camp opens"],["2004","Abu Ghraib photographs published"],["2014","US Senate report on CIA interrogations"]],
s2:[["The ticking bomb argument","Some argue torture could be justified if a captured terrorist knew where a bomb was about to explode. Critics answer that such situations almost never occur in reality, that torturers rarely know who has information, and that allowing exceptions leads to widespread abuse."],["Helping survivors","Survivors of torture often suffer long-term physical pain, nightmares, anxiety and depression. Specialized treatment centers around the world, many funded by the UN Voluntary Fund for Victims of Torture, provide medical, psychological and legal help."]],
sp:["‘The question’","In medieval and early modern Europe, torture was called ‘the question’ because it was used during interrogation. Instruments such as the rack were displayed to frighten prisoners; many confessed at the sight of them."],
g:[["Torture","Deliberately causing severe pain or suffering"],["Interrogation","Formal questioning, often by police or soldiers"],["Inadmissible","Not allowed to be used as evidence in court"],["Detainee","A person held in custody"]],
f:["Torture is banned under international law in all circumstances.","Prussia abolished judicial torture in 1754.","About 780 men were held at Guantanamo Bay over the years."],
fun:"During World War II, the German interrogator Hanns Scharff became famous for getting information from captured pilots through friendly walks, conversation and coffee rather than force."});

ADDART({id:"pseudoscience",t:"Pseudoscience",c:"Science",i:"🔮",w:"Pseudoscience",r:["scimeth","conspiracy","antivax","scope","illusions"],
p:[["Not real science","Pseudoscience is a belief or practice that claims to be scientific but is not."],["Common examples","Astrology, homeopathy and phrenology are well-known examples."],["Spotting it","Real science makes testable claims and changes with new evidence."]],
l:"Pseudoscience is a set of beliefs or practices that claim to be scientific but do not follow the scientific method. Common examples include astrology, which claims that the positions of stars and planets at birth shape personality and destiny; homeopathy, which treats illnesses with remedies diluted so much that usually no molecules of the original substance remain; and phrenology, a 19th-century theory that the shape of the skull reveals character. Pseudosciences often rely on anecdotes and testimonials rather than controlled experiments, make claims that cannot be tested or disproved, ignore contrary evidence and do not change when proved wrong. The philosopher Karl Popper argued that real scientific theories must be ‘falsifiable’, meaning that some possible observation could show them to be wrong. Pseudoscience can be harmless entertainment, but it can also be dangerous, for example when people abandon effective medical treatment. Teaching critical thinking helps people evaluate such claims.",
s:[["Famous examples","Astrology dates back thousands of years and was once linked with astronomy. Homeopathy was invented by the German doctor Samuel Hahnemann in 1796. Phrenology, popularized by Franz Joseph Gall around 1800, was used to justify racism and is now discredited. Other examples include numerology, dowsing for water and claims about ‘energy healing’."],["Testing the claims","In a 1985 study published in Nature, astrologers were unable to match personality profiles to birth charts better than chance. In 2015, Australia’s National Health and Medical Research Council reviewed the evidence and found no reliable evidence that homeopathy works for any health condition. Such studies use controls, blinding and statistics to rule out chance and bias."],["Why people believe","Pseudoscience often appeals because it offers simple explanations, personal attention and hope. The placebo effect, in which people feel better because they expect to, and natural recovery from illness can make useless treatments seem to work. People also tend to remember hits and forget misses, a bias called confirmation bias."],["Harm","Pseudoscience can cost money and time, and can be dangerous when people refuse vaccines or delay cancer treatment for unproven remedies. Regulators in many countries require health products to be backed by evidence, and some public health systems have stopped funding homeopathy."]],
k:[["Definition","Claims presented as science that do not follow scientific methods"],["Key test","Falsifiability (Karl Popper)"],["Examples","Astrology, homeopathy, phrenology"],["Homeopathy invented","1796, Samuel Hahnemann"],["Australian homeopathy review","2015, no reliable evidence"]],
d:[["1796","Hahnemann invents homeopathy"],["c. 1800","Gall develops phrenology"],["1934","Popper proposes falsifiability"],["1964","James Randi offers a prize for proof of the paranormal"],["1985","Nature study tests astrology"],["2015","Australia’s review of homeopathy"]],
s2:[["The million-dollar challenge","The magician and skeptic James Randi offered a prize, eventually one million dollars, to anyone who could demonstrate a paranormal ability under agreed test conditions. From 1964 to 2015, more than a thousand people applied, but no one passed even the preliminary test."],["Signs of pseudoscience","Warning signs include claims that sound too good to be true, reliance on testimonials, use of scientific-sounding words without clear meaning, claims of persecution by ‘mainstream science’ and refusal to allow independent testing."]],
sp:["The Barnum effect","People tend to rate vague personality descriptions, such as ‘you have a great deal of unused potential’, as highly accurate for themselves. Named after the showman P. T. Barnum, this effect helps explain why horoscopes and fortune-tellers seem convincing."],
g:[["Pseudoscience","False science that imitates real science"],["Falsifiable","Able to be shown false by evidence"],["Placebo","A fake treatment that can still make people feel better"],["Confirmation bias","Noticing evidence that supports what we already believe"]],
f:["Homeopathic remedies are often so diluted that none of the original substance remains.","Phrenology claimed skull bumps revealed character.","No one ever passed James Randi’s paranormal challenge."],
fun:"In a famous 1948 experiment, psychologist Bertram Forer gave students the same vague personality description, and they rated it as highly accurate for themselves."});

ADDART({id:"cryptids",t:"Cryptids: Bigfoot, Nessie and Other Mystery Animals",c:"Nature",i:"🦶",w:"Cryptid",r:["pseudoscience","conspiracy","ufos","squid","scimeth"],
p:[["Legendary creatures","Cryptids are animals whose existence is claimed but not proven, such as Bigfoot and the Loch Ness Monster."],["No solid evidence","Photos and sightings have not been confirmed by scientific proof."],["Real surprises","Some animals once thought mythical, like the giant squid, turned out to be real."]],
l:"A cryptid is an animal whose existence is claimed, usually based on folklore, sightings or blurry photographs, but has not been proven by science. Famous examples include the Loch Ness Monster (‘Nessie’) in Scotland, Bigfoot or Sasquatch in North America, the Yeti of the Himalayas and the chupacabra of the Americas. The study of such creatures, called cryptozoology, is not considered a science by most biologists, because it starts from belief in a particular creature rather than testing evidence. Investigations have found that many famous pieces of evidence were hoaxes or misidentifications: the best-known photo of Nessie, taken in 1934, was revealed in 1994 to be a toy submarine with a model head. Yet nature still holds surprises. The coelacanth, a fish thought extinct for 66 million years, was found alive in 1938, and the giant squid, source of sea-monster legends, was first filmed alive in its deep-sea home in 2012. Cryptids remain popular in folklore, tourism and films.",
s:[["The Loch Ness Monster","Reports of a creature in Loch Ness grew after a road was built along the loch in 1933. The ‘surgeon’s photograph’ of 1934 made Nessie famous. In 2018 scientists collected environmental DNA from the loch’s water and found no reptile DNA but large amounts of eel DNA, suggesting that some sightings might be large eels."],["Bigfoot","Stories of large, hairy wild people exist in many Native American traditions. The name Bigfoot became popular after large footprints were found in California in 1958; after the man responsible died in 2002, his family said he had made them as a prank. A famous 1967 film by Roger Patterson and Robert Gimlin shows a figure walking, which skeptics say is a person in a costume."],["The Yeti and others","Expeditions to the Himalayas collected ‘Yeti’ hair and bones; DNA studies in 2014 and 2017 found they came from bears and other known animals. The chupacabra, said to drink livestock blood, was first reported in Puerto Rico in 1995; animals found in later reports were usually coyotes or dogs with mange."],["Animals once unknown to science","The okapi, a forest relative of the giraffe, was unknown to European scientists until 1901, and the Vu Quang ox, or saola, was discovered in Vietnam and Laos in 1992. Such discoveries keep people curious, but scientists stress that new species are confirmed with specimens and DNA, not sightings alone."]],
k:[["Famous cryptids","Loch Ness Monster, Bigfoot, Yeti, chupacabra"],["Field","Cryptozoology (not considered a science)"],["‘Surgeon’s photograph’ revealed as hoax","1994"],["Coelacanth rediscovered","1938"],["Giant squid first filmed in the deep sea","2012"]],
d:[["1901","Okapi described by scientists"],["1933","Modern Loch Ness sightings begin"],["1938","Coelacanth found alive"],["1958","‘Bigfoot’ footprints in California"],["1967","Patterson–Gimlin film"],["1994","Nessie photo exposed as a hoax"],["2012","Giant squid filmed alive in the deep sea"]],
s2:[["Why people see monsters","Logs, waves, otters, swimming deer, boat wakes and shadows can look like creatures, especially from a distance. People who expect to see a monster are more likely to interpret unclear sights as one. Photos taken without scale make objects hard to judge."],["Cryptids and tourism","The Loch Ness Monster brings millions of pounds to the Scottish Highlands each year, and towns in the United States hold Bigfoot festivals. Even scientists who doubt the creatures exist often enjoy the stories as part of local culture."]],
sp:["The coelacanth","In 1938, museum curator Marjorie Courtenay-Latimer spotted a strange blue fish among a fisherman’s catch in South Africa. It was a coelacanth, a group known only from fossils and believed extinct since the age of dinosaurs. A second species was found in Indonesia in 1997."],
g:[["Cryptid","An animal whose existence is claimed but not proven"],["Cryptozoology","The search for cryptids"],["Hoax","A deliberate trick"],["Environmental DNA","Traces of DNA left by living things in water or soil"]],
f:["The famous 1934 photo of Nessie was a toy submarine with a model head.","The coelacanth was thought extinct for 66 million years.","DNA tests on ‘Yeti’ samples found bears and other known animals."],
fun:"In 2023, the largest organized search for the Loch Ness Monster in 50 years used drones, thermal cameras and an underwater microphone, but found no monster."});

ADDART({id:"billionaires",t:"Billionaires and Extreme Wealth",c:"Society",i:"💰",w:"Billionaire",r:["inequality","taxhavens","taxes","capitalism","populism"],
p:[["Record fortunes","In 2026, Forbes counted a record 3,428 billionaires."],["How fortunes are made","Most billionaires made money through companies, especially technology."],["A heated debate","People disagree about whether extreme wealth is earned reward or a sign of an unfair system."]],
l:"A billionaire is a person whose wealth is worth at least one billion US dollars, a thousand million. According to Forbes magazine’s 2026 list, there were a record 3,428 billionaires in the world, with combined wealth of more than 20 trillion dollars, and Elon Musk was the richest person. Most billionaires live in the United States, China and India, and many made their fortunes by founding or investing in companies, especially in technology, finance, retail and energy, while others inherited wealth. Supporters argue that billionaires’ fortunes reflect the value of products and jobs they created, that most of their wealth is invested in companies, and that many give large sums to charity. Critics argue that extreme wealth concentrates economic and political power, that it often comes from monopolies, low taxes or inherited advantage, and that it coexists with poverty. Proposals such as wealth taxes and a global minimum tax on billionaires are hotly debated.",
s:[["The first billionaires","John D. Rockefeller, founder of Standard Oil, is often described as the first American billionaire, around 1916. Other famous fortunes of the industrial age belonged to Andrew Carnegie and Cornelius Vanderbilt. Forbes began publishing its list of billionaires in 1987, when it counted 140."],["Where wealth comes from","Founders of technology companies such as Amazon, Microsoft, Google, Meta, Nvidia and Tesla hold much of their wealth as company shares, so their fortunes rise and fall with stock markets. In some countries, fortunes have been built through close links with governments or the privatization of state companies."],["Philanthropy","In 2010 Bill Gates, Melinda French Gates and Warren Buffett launched the Giving Pledge, inviting billionaires to promise to give away most of their wealth. Foundations funded by billionaires support health, education and research, though critics say they give unelected individuals great influence over public priorities."],["Taxing the rich","Some countries have wealth taxes, though several abolished them because the wealthy moved away or hid assets. In 2024, Brazil proposed a global minimum tax of 2 percent on billionaires’ wealth at the G20. Supporters say it would fund public services; opponents argue it would be hard to collect and would discourage investment."]],
k:[["Billionaires (Forbes 2026)","3,428, a record"],["Combined wealth","Over 20 trillion US dollars"],["Richest person (2026)","Elon Musk"],["First Forbes list","1987, 140 billionaires"],["Giving Pledge","2010"]],
d:[["1916","Rockefeller often called the first billionaire"],["1987","Forbes publishes its first billionaires list"],["2010","Giving Pledge launched"],["2021","Global minimum corporate tax agreed"],["2024","Brazil proposes a billionaire tax at the G20"],["2026","Record 3,428 billionaires"]],
s2:[["How big is a billion?","If you earned 1,000 dollars a day, it would take about 2,740 years to earn a billion dollars. A stack of one billion one-dollar bills would be about 109 kilometers high."],["Inequality debate","Charities such as Oxfam report that the richest 1 percent own a large share of global wealth. Economists debate whether this matters if poverty is falling overall, and what policies, such as education, taxes or competition rules, best create shared prosperity."]],
sp:["Andrew Carnegie’s gospel","In 1889, the steel tycoon Andrew Carnegie wrote that ‘the man who dies thus rich dies disgraced’, arguing that the wealthy should use their fortunes for the public good. He funded more than 2,500 public libraries around the world."],
g:[["Billionaire","A person with wealth of at least one billion dollars"],["Philanthropy","Giving money to good causes"],["Wealth tax","A yearly tax on the total value of what a person owns"],["Monopoly","A company that controls an entire market"]],
f:["Forbes counted a record 3,428 billionaires in 2026.","Andrew Carnegie funded over 2,500 public libraries.","Earning 1,000 dollars a day, it would take about 2,740 years to make a billion."],
fun:"A million seconds is about 11.5 days, but a billion seconds is about 31.7 years."});

ADDART({id:"affirmativeaction",t:"Affirmative Action",c:"Society",i:"⚖️",w:"Affirmative action",r:["racism","segregation","education","universities","feminism"],
p:[["Correcting inequality","Affirmative action gives preference to disadvantaged groups in jobs or education."],["Used worldwide","India, the United States, Malaysia and South Africa have used such policies."],["Fierce debate","Supporters see fairness; opponents see unfair discrimination."]],
l:"Affirmative action, also called positive discrimination or reservation in some countries, refers to policies that give preference or special opportunities to members of groups that have faced discrimination, such as racial or ethnic minorities, women or lower castes, in areas like university admissions, jobs and government contracts. India’s constitution of 1950 reserved places in universities, government jobs and legislatures for Scheduled Castes and Scheduled Tribes, one of the world’s largest such systems. In the United States, President John F. Kennedy used the phrase in 1961, and universities and employers adopted policies to increase diversity, until the Supreme Court ruled race-conscious university admissions unconstitutional in 2023. Malaysia and South Africa have also used preferences, for the Malay majority and for Black South Africans. Supporters argue such policies correct past and present injustice and create more diverse institutions. Opponents argue that they treat individuals unfairly based on group identity, can benefit the already advantaged within groups and may stigmatize beneficiaries.",
s:[["India","India’s reservations aim to reverse centuries of caste discrimination. They were later extended to Other Backward Classes in 1990 and to economically weaker sections in 2019. In 1992 the Supreme Court generally capped reservations at 50 percent. Reservations have helped millions but remain politically contested."],["The United States","Kennedy’s 1961 order required federal contractors to take ‘affirmative action’ against discrimination. In Bakke (1978), the Supreme Court banned racial quotas but allowed race as one factor in admissions, which Grutter (2003) upheld. On 29 June 2023, in Students for Fair Admissions v. Harvard, the Court ruled that race-conscious admissions violated the Constitution."],["Malaysia and South Africa","Malaysia’s New Economic Policy of 1971 favored the ethnic Malay and Indigenous majority, known as bumiputera, in education, business and housing, aiming to reduce poverty after ethnic riots in 1969. South Africa’s Black Economic Empowerment policies since 2003 aim to undo the economic effects of apartheid."],["Arguments","Supporters say equal treatment is not enough when people start from unequal positions, and that diverse classrooms and workplaces benefit everyone. Opponents argue for policies based on economic need rather than race or ethnicity, saying merit should decide and that group preferences can cause resentment."]],
k:[["Also called","Positive discrimination, reservation"],["India’s reservations","Constitution of 1950"],["US phrase first used","1961, Executive Order 10925"],["US Supreme Court ruling","29 June 2023, race-conscious admissions ended"],["Malaysia’s New Economic Policy","1971"]],
d:[["1950","India’s constitution creates reservations"],["1961","Kennedy uses the phrase ‘affirmative action’"],["1971","Malaysia’s New Economic Policy"],["1978","Bakke case bans racial quotas in the USA"],["2003","South Africa’s Black Economic Empowerment law"],["2023","US Supreme Court ends race-conscious admissions"]],
s2:[["Women and affirmative action","Some countries use quotas to increase women’s representation, such as requirements for women on company boards in Norway (2003) and gender quotas for candidates in many parliaments. Supporters say they break barriers; critics say they can be seen as tokenism."],["Class-based approaches","Some universities give extra consideration to applicants from poor families, rural areas or schools that rarely send students to university. Such policies are often more popular than race-based ones but may not produce the same diversity."]],
sp:["B. R. Ambedkar","Bhimrao Ramji Ambedkar, born into a caste considered ‘untouchable’, overcame discrimination to earn doctorates abroad and chaired the committee that drafted India’s constitution. He championed reservations to give oppressed castes access to education and power."],
g:[["Affirmative action","Policies giving preference to disadvantaged groups"],["Quota","A fixed number or share reserved for a group"],["Caste","A hereditary social class in South Asia"],["Diversity","Including people of different backgrounds"]],
f:["India has one of the world’s largest affirmative action systems.","The US Supreme Court ended race-conscious university admissions in 2023.","Malaysia’s policy favors the majority, not a minority."],
fun:"B. R. Ambedkar, architect of India’s constitution, owned a personal library of tens of thousands of books, one of the largest private collections in Asia in his time."});

ADDART({id:"hatespeech",t:"Hate Speech",c:"Society",i:"🗯️",w:"Hate speech",r:["censorship","racism","genocide","antisemitism","hrights"],
p:[["Attacking identity","Hate speech attacks people because of their race, religion, ethnicity, gender or other identity."],["Different laws","Some countries ban it; others, like the United States, protect most speech."],["Online challenge","Social media has made hate speech spread faster and wider."]],
l:"Hate speech is speech, writing or images that attack, insult or incite hatred or violence against people based on characteristics such as race, ethnicity, religion, nationality, gender, sexual orientation or disability. There is no single international definition, and laws differ widely. Many countries, especially in Europe, criminalize some forms, such as inciting hatred or, in several countries, denying the Holocaust. The United States protects most offensive speech under the First Amendment, allowing restrictions only on speech intended and likely to provoke imminent violence, true threats and a few other categories. International law requires countries to prohibit advocacy of national, racial or religious hatred that amounts to incitement to discrimination, hostility or violence. History shows that hate propaganda has preceded atrocities, from Nazi Germany to the Rwandan genocide. Debates focus on balancing protection from harm against freedom of expression, who decides what counts as hate speech, and how social media companies should moderate content.",
s:[["Words before violence","Nazi propaganda, such as the newspaper Der Stürmer, portrayed Jews as dangerous; its publisher Julius Streicher was executed after the Nuremberg trials. In Rwanda, the radio station RTLM called Tutsi ‘cockroaches’ and broadcast names of people to kill in 1994; in 2003 an international tribunal convicted its founders of incitement to genocide."],["Laws around the world","Germany bans incitement to hatred and Nazi symbols, and its 2017 Network Enforcement Act required large platforms to remove clearly illegal content quickly. The EU’s Digital Services Act, fully applied from 2024, requires platforms to tackle illegal content. In the United States, the Supreme Court’s 1969 Brandenburg v. Ohio decision set a high bar for punishing speech."],["Online hate","Social media allows hateful content to spread instantly and anonymously, and algorithms can amplify outrage. Platforms use rules, human moderators and artificial intelligence to remove content, but face criticism both for leaving harmful posts up and for removing legitimate speech. In Myanmar, UN investigators found that Facebook posts had helped spread hatred against the Rohingya."],["The debate","Supporters of hate speech laws argue they protect vulnerable groups, prevent violence and uphold dignity. Opponents warn that vague laws can be used to silence minorities, critics and journalists, and that counter-speech, education and open debate are better responses. Many agree that direct incitement to violence should be banned."]],
k:[["Common targets","Race, religion, ethnicity, gender, sexuality, disability"],["US standard","Brandenburg v. Ohio (1969)"],["Germany’s platform law","Network Enforcement Act (2017)"],["UN guidance","Rabat Plan of Action (2012)"],["International day","18 June, Day for Countering Hate Speech"]],
d:[["1946","Julius Streicher executed after Nuremberg"],["1966","UN covenant requires bans on incitement to hatred"],["1969","Brandenburg v. Ohio"],["1994","RTLM broadcasts in Rwanda"],["2003","Rwanda tribunal convicts media leaders"],["2017","Germany’s Network Enforcement Act"],["2022","UN’s first International Day for Countering Hate Speech"]],
s2:[["Counter-speech","Counter-speech means responding to hateful messages with facts, humor or messages of solidarity rather than censorship. Research suggests it can reduce the spread of hate online, especially when bystanders show that hateful views are not the norm."],["Where to draw the line","Courts often consider who is speaking, to whom, in what context, with what intent, and how likely harm is. The UN’s Rabat Plan of Action lists six such factors for deciding when speech becomes incitement."]],
sp:["The Media Case","In the ‘Media Case’ of 2003, the International Criminal Tribunal for Rwanda convicted the founders of RTLM radio and the editor of the newspaper Kangura of genocide and incitement. It was the first international trial since Nuremberg to convict journalists for their role in atrocities."],
g:[["Hate speech","Expression that attacks people for their identity"],["Incitement","Encouraging others to commit violence or crimes"],["Content moderation","Reviewing and removing posts on platforms"],["Counter-speech","Answering hateful speech with other speech"]],
f:["There is no single international definition of hate speech.","Rwandan radio broadcasts helped incite the 1994 genocide.","The United States protects most offensive speech under the First Amendment."],
fun:"Some online games and forums use ‘shadow banning’, in which a person’s hateful posts are hidden from everyone else without them realizing it."});

ADDART({id:"cloning",t:"Cloning",c:"Science",i:"🐑",w:"Cloning",r:["crispr","gmos","eugenics","dna"],
p:[["Genetic copies","Cloning creates a genetically identical copy of an organizm or cell."],["Dolly the sheep","In 1996, Dolly became the first mammal cloned from an adult cell."],["Human cloning","Reproductive human cloning is banned or opposed almost everywhere."]],
l:"Cloning is the process of creating a genetically identical copy of a cell, tissue or whole organizm. It happens naturally: identical twins are clones, and many plants and bacteria reproduce by cloning. Scientists clone genes and cells routinely in research and medicine. Cloning a whole animal is usually done by somatic cell nuclear transfer, in which the nucleus of an adult cell is placed into an egg whose own nucleus has been removed. In 1996, scientists in Scotland created Dolly the sheep, the first mammal cloned from an adult cell. Since then, cattle, pigs, dogs, cats, horses and monkeys have been cloned, and companies offer to clone pets. Cloning may help save endangered species and produce research animals. However, success rates are low, and cloned animals can have health problems. Cloning a human being is banned or opposed in most countries because of safety and ethical concerns, though ‘therapeutic cloning’ to make cells for research is debated.",
s:[["Early experiments","In the 1950s and 1960s, scientists cloned frogs; John Gurdon showed in 1962 that the nucleus of a mature frog cell could produce a whole new tadpole. He shared the 2012 Nobel Prize in Medicine with Shinya Yamanaka, who found a way to turn adult cells back into stem cells."],["Dolly","Dolly was born on 5 July 1996 at the Roslin Institute near Edinburgh, from a cell taken from a sheep’s udder; she was one success out of 277 attempts. Announced in February 1997, she caused worldwide excitement and alarm. She had six lambs and was put down in 2003 because of a lung disease."],["Cloning since Dolly","Scientists cloned the first dog, Snuppy, in South Korea in 2005, and the first primates, two macaques named Zhong Zhong and Hua Hua, in China in 2018. In 2020, an endangered black-footed ferret named Elizabeth Ann was cloned from cells frozen in 1988, offering hope for genetic rescue of rare species."],["Ethics","Concerns include animal welfare, because many cloned embryos fail, and the idea of copying people. In 2005 the UN General Assembly adopted a non-binding declaration urging countries to ban human cloning. Claims by groups to have cloned humans have never been verified. A clone would be a separate person with its own experiences, not a copy of someone’s mind."]],
k:[["Method","Somatic cell nuclear transfer"],["First cloned mammal from an adult cell","Dolly the sheep, born 5 July 1996"],["First cloned primates","Zhong Zhong and Hua Hua, 2018"],["UN declaration on human cloning","2005 (non-binding)"],["Natural clones","Identical twins"]],
d:[["1962","John Gurdon clones frogs from adult cells"],["1996","Dolly the sheep is born"],["1997","Dolly announced to the world"],["2003","Dolly dies"],["2005","First cloned dog; UN declaration on human cloning"],["2018","First cloned monkeys"],["2020","Endangered black-footed ferret cloned"]],
s2:[["Therapeutic cloning","Therapeutic cloning would create embryos as a source of stem cells genetically matched to a patient, which could treat diseases without rejection. It raises ethical objections because embryos are destroyed. Induced pluripotent stem cells, made from adult cells without embryos, now offer an alternative."],["Cloned food","Cloned cattle and pigs are mostly used to breed animals with desirable traits, and their offspring may enter the food supply. Regulators in the United States have said food from clones and their offspring is safe, while the European Union has been more cautious."]],
sp:["Why ‘Dolly’?","Dolly was cloned from a cell taken from a sheep’s mammary gland, and the scientists named her after the country singer Dolly Parton, known for her large bust. Parton reportedly took the joke in good humor."],
g:[["Clone","A genetically identical copy"],["Nucleus","The part of a cell containing its DNA"],["Stem cell","A cell that can develop into many types of cells"],["Embryo","An organizm in its earliest stage of development"]],
f:["Identical twins are natural clones.","Dolly was the only success out of 277 attempts.","A clone would be a separate person, not a copy of someone’s memories."],
fun:"The Argentine polo star Adolfo Cambiaso has ridden several clones of his favorite mare, Cuartetera, in top matches."});
